==============================================================================
VERDICT
==============================================================================
  Every S_j, j = 1..14, carries a certified interior zero.
  Theorem A's hard half SURVIVES on its own near-boundary family, far
  past the |S| <= 6 certification -- and now with no truncation caveat.

  |z_min| trajectory:
     j = 1    |z_min| = 0.781546   1 - |z_min| = 2.185e-01
     j = 2    |z_min| = 0.880050   1 - |z_min| = 1.200e-01
     j = 3    |z_min| = 0.910086   1 - |z_min| = 8.991e-02
     j = 4    |z_min| = 0.926198   1 - |z_min| = 7.380e-02
     j = 5    |z_min| = 0.941139   1 - |z_min| = 5.886e-02
     j = 6    |z_min| = 0.949660   1 - |z_min| = 5.034e-02
     j = 7    |z_min| = 0.955194   1 - |z_min| = 4.481e-02
     j = 8    |z_min| = 0.961059   1 - |z_min| = 3.894e-02
     j = 9    |z_min| = 0.964993   1 - |z_min| = 3.501e-02
     j = 10   |z_min| = 0.967770   1 - |z_min| = 3.223e-02
     j = 11   |z_min| = 0.970890   1 - |z_min| = 2.911e-02
     j = 12   |z_min| = 0.972058   1 - |z_min| = 2.794e-02
     j = 13   |z_min| = 0.972328   1 - |z_min| = 2.767e-02
     j = 14   |z_min| = 0.972325   1 - |z_min| = 2.767e-02

  The zero approaches the boundary but does not reach it; the
  certification ceiling at |S| <= 6 was an artifact of the truncated-
  series instrument, not a boundary in the mathematics.  The product
  form removes it and extends the certificate by a factor of ~2 in j.
